  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "In nineteen sixty-eight, a 3M chemist tried to make an extremely strong glue, and failed.",
      "start": 0.12,
      "end": 5.72
    },
    {
      "text": "Spencer Silver had made the opposite.",
      "start": 6.2,
      "end": 7.8
    },
    {
      "text": "A glue so weak, you could peel it off and stick it back again.",
      "start": 8.4,
      "end": 11.9
    },
    {
      "text": "Nobody wanted it.",
      "start": 12.46,
      "end": 13.44
    },
    {
      "text": "For five years, he showed it to anyone who'd listen.",
      "start": 13.86,
      "end": 16.24
    },
    {
      "text": "Then a colleague, Art Fry, had a problem in church.",
      "start": 16.78,
      "end": 19.3
    },
    {
      "text": "His paper bookmarks kept falling out of his hymn book.",
      "start": 19.6,
      "end": 22.3
    },
    {
      "text": "So he coated them in Silver's weak glue.",
      "start": 22.65,
      "end": 24.79
    },
    {
      "text": "They stayed, and they peeled off clean.",
      "start": 25.31,
      "end": 27.07
    },
    {
      "text": "But the first time 3M sold them, in four cities, they flopped.",
      "start": 27.57,
      "end": 31.09
    },
    {
      "text": "They were called Press 'n Peel.",
      "start": 31.47,
      "end": 33.01
    },
    {
      "text": "In nineteen eighty, they came back as Post-it Notes.",
      "start": 33.57,
      "end": 35.73
    },
    {
      "text": "He was trying to make glue that never lets go.",
      "start": 36.23,
      "end": 38.47
    },
    {
      "text": "It's on your fridge because it always does.",
      "start": 39.07,
      "end": 41.15
    }
  ],
